Bootstrap: docker
From: python:3.13-slim

%labels
    Author seq2fun
    Version 1.1
    Description seq2fun CPU (x86_64): PyTorch 2.8.0 CPU build, same stack as seq2fun.sif

%help
    The x86_64 companion to seq2fun.sif, which is arm64 and runs only on the
    gh200 GPU nodes. This one runs on the login node and the CPU partitions,
    so model code and analysis code can be exercised without a GPU
    allocation.

    Library versions are pinned to the GPU image so behaviour matches; only
    the torch wheel differs (CPU instead of CUDA 12.9). It also carries the
    analysis stack the bare CPU nodes lack -- scikit-learn above all, which
    is why AUROC has had to be hand-rolled as a rank statistic.

        apptainer exec --bind /cluster:/cluster seq2fun_cpu.sif python my_analysis.py

    Unlike seq2fun.def this image is pip-only, with no conda layer. micromamba
    segfaults during solve under the emulated fakeroot these nodes build with
    (semop: Invalid argument), and the conda-only tools it supplied --
    samtools, bcftools, bedtools -- are never invoked by this project, which
    reads FASTA through pysam.

%environment
    export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1
    export MPLBACKEND=Agg

%post
    set -eux
    pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip

    # CPU wheel. Same torch version as the GPU image so the model code behaves
    # identically; torchvision and torchaudio are left out, nothing imports them.
    pip install --no-cache-dir torch==2.8.0 \
        --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu

    # Versions pinned to seq2fun.def where that file pins them.
    pip install --no-cache-dir \
        transformers==5.3.0 \
        accelerate==1.13.0 \
        scikit-learn==1.8.0 \
        zarr==3.1.5 \
        "python-dotenv>=1.0.0" \
        pysam \
        wandb \
        datasets \
        fire \
        tqdm \
        scipy \
        matplotlib \
        pandas \
        pyarrow \
        joblib \
        pytest

%test
    # A failing %test marks the build failed, so these are the properties the
    # image is useless without rather than a broad smoke test.
    python -c "import platform; assert platform.machine()=='x86_64', platform.machine()"
    python -c "import torch; print('torch', torch.__version__); assert not torch.cuda.is_available()"
    python -c "import sklearn, scipy, pandas, numpy, pyarrow, matplotlib, datasets, pysam, transformers, pytest; print('imports ok')"
    python - <<'PY'
import torch, torch.nn.functional as F
# the N encoding the project depends on: index 0 is N and must carry no channel
x = F.one_hot(torch.tensor([[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]]).long(), 5).float()[..., 1:]
assert x[0, 0].sum() == 0, x[0, 0]
assert x[0, 1].tolist() == [1, 0, 0, 0]
print("tokens_to_onehot semantics ok")
PY
